    "points": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "First conjugation (-ar / cantar)",
        "body": "What do you think you will discover today about “Spanish verbs: -ar, -er, and -ir”?\n\nA Spanish verb names an action. To **conjugate** means to change it for the person who acts. An **infinitive** is the dictionary form, such as **cantar**. The **stem** remains after you drop **-ar**; the **ending** is what you add.\n\nMira este ejemplo. **Cantar** belongs to the **-ar** family. With stem **cant-**: yo **canto**, tú **cantas**, él **canta**. **Hablar** follows the same pattern: hablo, hablas, habla.\n\nPractice: Now it's your turn. Your turn. Write yo / tú / él for cantar and hablar. Say them out loud.\n\nCommon mistake: Keeping the -ar on the stem (cantaro). Drop -ar first, then add the ending."
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "heading": "Second conjugation (-er / comer)",
        "body": "**Comer** is an infinitive in the Spanish **-er** family. Its stem is **com-**. Present endings for yo, tú, and él are **-o**, **-es**, and **-e**.\n\nMira este ejemplo. Com- plus -o makes yo **como**; plus -es makes tú **comes**; plus -e makes él **come**. **Beber** works the same way: bebo, bebes, bebe. Notice -er and -ir share -o/-es/-e for these three persons, but the infinitive ending still shows the family.\n\nPractice: Now it's your turn. Your turn. Write yo / tú / él for comer and beber. Circle the endings.\n\nCommon mistake: Using -as/-a (from -ar) with comer. Say comes / come, not comas / coma in this present pattern."
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "heading": "Third conjugation (-ir / vivir)",
        "body": "**Vivir** is an infinitive in the Spanish **-ir** family. Its stem is **viv-**. For yo, tú, and él the present endings are again **-o**, **-es**, and **-e**: **vivo**, **vives**, **vive**.\n\nMira este ejemplo. **Escribir** follows the same route: escribo, escribes, escribe. Compare three infinitives side by side: cantar / comer / vivir. The family label sits on the infinitive; the person shows in the ending.\n\nPractice: Now it's your turn. Your turn. Make a three-column chart for cantar, comer, and vivir (yo, tú, él). Draw one clear symbol or small scene about “Spanish verbs: -ar, -er, and -ir”. Add one label from today's lesson.\n\nCommon mistake: Mixing families: writing «yo vive» or «él canto». Match stem family and person ending.\n\n## Drawing for the image band\nDraw one clear symbol or small scene about “Spanish verbs: -ar, -er, and -ir”. Add one label from today's lesson."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "Three Spanish verb families: -ar, -er, and -ir. Conjugate cantar, comer, and vivir for yo, tú, and él.\n\n## Drawing for the image band\nDraw one clear symbol or small scene about “Spanish verbs: -ar, -er, and -ir”. Add one label from today's lesson.",
